## Conversation
The user asked for a minimal DFlash2 implementation based on the upstream repository and TorchSpec DFlash and DSpark pull requests, with tests and a draft pull request. Final test and upstream-parity reviews found uncovered serving callers, Qwen3 configuration normalization gaps, and unsupported distributed/configuration modes that needed explicit boundaries.

## Actions
Normalized omitted Qwen3 layer types and the 4096-token sliding-window default, preserved official target-depth semantics when the nested DFlash2 schema captures every layer, and rejected unsupported activations, attention bias/dropout, scaled RoPE, empty or invalid target-layer lists, and USP training. Hardened tests for nonzero finish-stage dynamic kernels, already-present selector gold tokens, online and offline serving artifacts, and exact tensor values. Ran the 18 focused tests, the 107-test related regression set with the local Mooncake import stub, Ruff, formatting, and diff checks. The upstream-parity reviewer reported no remaining findings.

## Errors & Mistakes
None.

## Dead Ends
Supporting mixed full-attention and sliding-attention draft layers would require per-layer block masks instead of the shared DFlash mask. The current wrapper fails fast on mixed layer types. Implementing arbitrary Qwen3 activations, attention biases, and scaled RoPE would broaden this change into a Qwen3 backbone rewrite, so unsupported non-defaults fail at configuration load instead.

## Hints for Future Agents
Qwen3 sets sliding_window to 4096 when use_sliding_window is true and to None otherwise, even when layer_types is explicit. Nested dflash_config.target_layer_ids identifies the official schema, where top-level num_target_layers is total target depth. Keep online Trainer saving and offline convert_to_hf output identical in both keys and tensor values. DFlash2 does not support USP until selector normalization uses the same gradient-sync group.

## Codebase Discoveries
TorchSpec DFlash uses SiLU, bias-free attention projections, zero attention dropout, and theta-only default RoPE regardless of extra Qwen3 config fields. The model-owned serving hooks now feed both the generic online writer and the offline converter. A Mooncake import-only stub is sufficient for CPU tests on macOS ARM because these tests do not exercise the transport.

## Open Questions
Mixed per-layer masks, non-default Qwen3 architecture flags, USP normalization, CUDA multi-rank execution, and convergence validation remain outside this pull request.
